executable to check architectures (x86_64, arm64).\n\n#### Phase 3: Extraction & Decompilation Strategy\nBased on the runtime identified in Phase 2:\n- **If Electron/JS:**\n  - Extract `.asar` archives using `npx @electron/asar extract`.\n  - Format, de-obfuscate, and restore module layout for all extracted JavaScript/TypeScript files.\n- **If Native (Swift / Objective-C):**\n  - Run `class-dump` or `dsdump` to extract Objective-C / Swift runtime class interfaces, ivars, protocols, and method signatures into corresponding header files (`.h`).\n  - Extract string references, imported symbols, and export tables using `otool`, `nm`, or `strings`.\n  - Use decompilation analysis (e.g., via Ghidra, Hopper, or IDA Pro) to reconstruct control flow, core logic, and model structures into idiomatic Swift or modern C/Objective-C equivalents.\n- **If UI Assets:**\n  - Convert asset catalogs (`Assets.car`) using `assetutil` or extraction tools to retrieve raw vector and raster assets.\n\n#### Phase 4: Project Scaffold & Reconstruction\n1. Construct a clean build configuration (`Package.swift`, `CMakeLists.txt`, `package.json`, or an `XcodeProj` structure matching the detected framework).\n2. Generate placeholder implementations and structural interfaces matching the discovered class hierarchies and API endpoints.\n3. Iteratively fill in the business logic, state management, and network layers based on the disassembled control flow.\n4. Document all missing dependencies, dynamic linking requirements, and binary-only third-party frameworks.\n\nBegin by requesting the target file path and terminal output for `hdiutil attach` and `file Contents/MacOS/<Executable>`.\n\n[Assistant thinking]: The user has attached files? They mention @Grok_Bot_0.44.0.dmg and @refrence/ — likely files in the working directory. Let me check the working directory to find them. This is a reverse engineering task — there's an ida-reverse skill and macos-reverse skill. The prompt says use skills when task ma